<h1>Variables &amp; types</h1>

<p>
  Values are bound via <code>let</code>. Kex infers the type and the REPL prints
  it after the colon, <code>name</code> is a <code>String</code>,
  <code>count</code> an <code>Int</code>, with no annotations needed.
</p>

<Code code={`let name = "kex"`} repl />

<Code code={`let count = 42`} repl />

<p>
  A <code>let</code> can't be reassigned. When you need something that changes, reach
  for <code>var</code> instead and then you can give it a new value:
</p>

<Code
  code={`var n = 5
n = n + 1
n`}
  repl
/>

<p>
  There's no <code>null</code> in kex. A value that might be missing is an
  <strong>Optional&lt;T&gt;</strong> which is usually shortened to <strong
    ><code>T?</code></strong
  >. It can either be <code>Just(value)</code> or <code>None</code>. The
  function <code>.or(default)</code>
  unwraps it, falling back when it's empty.
</p>

<Code code={`Just(5).or(0)`} repl />

<Code code={`None.or(0)`} repl />

<p>
  One more everyday type: <code>1..10</code> is a <strong>range</strong> (<strong
    ><code>Range&lt;Int&gt;</code></strong
  > in this example), every integer from 1 to 10 without allocating a list up front.
  Turn it into one with <code>.to(List)</code>, or work with it lazily using the
  same
  <code>map</code>/<code>filter</code>/<code>sum</code> methods you'll meet next.
</p>

<Code code={`(1..5).to(List)`} repl />
